the remote Docker engine address and a port&nbsp;
as shown here. For example, to run a container&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:17.810 --> 01:41:29.330
based on nginx on a remote Docker host, run the&nbsp;
command Docker dash H equals 10.1 23 dot 2.1 colon&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:29.330 --> 01:41:42.970
2375 run ngi nx. Now let's try and understand how&nbsp;
exactly our applications containerized in Docker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:42.970 --> 01:41:50.950
how does it work under the hood, Docker uses&nbsp;
namespaces to isolate workspace, process IDs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:50.950 --> 01:41:58.060
network, inter process communication, mounds&nbsp;
and Unix time sharing systems are created in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:58.060 --> 01:42:07.000
their own namespace thereby providing isolation&nbsp;
between containers. Let's take a look at one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:07.000 --> 01:42:13.210
the namespace isolation technique. process ID&nbsp;
namespaces. Whenever a Linux system boots up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:13.210 --> 01:42:19.690
it starts with just one process with a process&nbsp;
ID of one. This is the root process and kicks&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:19.690 --> 01:42:24.970
off all the other processes in the system.&nbsp;
By the time the system boots up completely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:24.970 --> 01:42:32.380
we have a handful of processes running. This can&nbsp;
be seen by running the PS command to list all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:32.380 --> 01:42:39.550
running processes. The process IDs are unique&nbsp;
and two processes cannot have the same process&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:39.550 --> 01:42:46.660
ID. Now if we were to create a container, which is&nbsp;
basically like a child system within the current&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:46.660 --> 01:42:53.320
system, the child system needs to think that it&nbsp;
is an independent system on its own. And it has&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:53.320 --> 01:43:01.780
its own set of processes originating from a root&nbsp;
process with a process ID of one. But we know that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:01.780 --> 01:43:07.600
there is no hard isolation between the containers&nbsp;
and the underlying host. So the processes running&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:07.600 --> 01:43:13.630
inside the container are in fact processes running&nbsp;
on the underlying host. And so two processes&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:13.630 --> 01:43:20.200
cannot have the same process ID of one. This is&nbsp;
where namespaces come into play. With process&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:20.200 --> 01:43:25.780
ID namespaces. Each process can have multiple&nbsp;
process IDs associated with it. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:25.780 --> 01:43:30.910
when the processes start in the container, it's&nbsp;
actually just another set of processes on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:30.910 --> 01:43:37.630
base Linux system. And it gets the next available&nbsp;
process ID in this case, five and six. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:37.630 --> 01:43:43.870
they also get another process ID starting with P&nbsp;
ID one in the container namespace which is only&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:43.870 --> 01:43:50.110
visible inside the container. So the container&nbsp;
things that it has its own route process tree. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:50.110 --> 01:43:57.530
so it is an independent system. So how does that&nbsp;
relate to an actual system? How do you see this on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:57.530 --> 01:44:04.970
a host? Let's say I were to run an nga next server&nbsp;
as a container. We know that the nginx container&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:04.970 --> 01:44:12.320
runs and nginx service. If we were to list all the&nbsp;
services inside the Docker container, we see that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:12.320 --> 01:44:20.210
the next service running with a process ID of one.&nbsp;
This is the process ID of the service inside of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:20.209 --> 01:44:26.749
the container namespace. If we list the services&nbsp;
on the Docker host, we will see the same service&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:26.750 --> 01:44:34.370
but with a different process ID that indicates&nbsp;
that all processes are in fact running on the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:34.370 --> 01:44:43.550
host but separated into their own containers using&nbsp;
namespaces. So we learned that the underlying&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:43.550 --> 01:44:51.560
Docker host as well as the containers share the&nbsp;
same system resources such as CPU and memory. How&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:51.560 --> 01:44:57.080
much of the resources are dedicated to the host&nbsp;
and the containers? And how does Docker manage&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:57.080 --> 01:45:03.590
and share the resources between the containers.&nbsp;
By default, there is no restriction as to how&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:03.590 --> 01:45:09.830
much of a resource a container can use. And&nbsp;
hence, a container may end up utilizing all&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:09.830 --> 01:45:16.010
of the resources on the underlying host. But there&nbsp;
is a way to restrict the amount of CPU or memory&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:16.010 --> 01:45:23.630
a container can use. Docker uses thi groups or&nbsp;
control groups to restrict the amount of hardware&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:23.629 --> 01:45:31.789
resources allocated to each container. This can&nbsp;
be done by providing the dash dash CPUs option&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:31.790 --> 01:45:37.850
to the Docker run command. Providing a value of&nbsp;
point five will ensure that the container does&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:37.850 --> 01:45:45.890
not take up more than 50% of the host CPU at any&nbsp;
given time. The same goes with memory. Setting a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:45.890 --> 01:45:51.980
value of 100 M to the dash dash memory option&nbsp;
limits the amount of memory the container can&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:51.979 --> 01:45:59.359
use to 100 megabytes. If you're interested in&nbsp;
reading more on this topic, refer to the links&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:59.359 --> 01:46:06.199
I posted in the reference page. That's it for now&nbsp;
on Docker engine. In the next lecture, we talked&nbsp;&nbsp;

